      "text": "The requested information has been gathered in steps:\n\n1.  **Publication Background and Reception:**\n    *   *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* by Stuart Turton was published in **2018** by **Sourcebooks Landmark**.\n    *   It is praised for its **recursive narrative** structure, exploration of **perspective and identity**, and blending of a **country house mystery** with a **time loop**. It has been widely reviewed in major outlets.\n\n2.  **Body-Hopping Time-Loop Premise Summary:**\n    *   The protagonist is forced to relive the same day eight times. Each time, they wake up inhabiting a different guest at the Blackheath estate (the novel has eight hosts). The goal is to solve the murder of Evelyn Hardcastle before the day resets or they fail to solve it in time. The structure forces the protagonist to view the events and characters from multiple, shifting perspectives.\n\n3.  **Accessibility via Digital Libraries (ISBN/Identifiers):**\n    *   The ISBN identified is **978-1-4926-6854-6**. While this confirms commercial availability, I cannot directly check institutional digital libraries using this API.\n\n4.  **Full Text Retrieval:**\n    *   I have successfully retrieved an **excerpt** of the complete plaintext from the book, beginning with Day One.\n    *   **Note:** While I used `compacted_get_book_text__compacted_get_book_text` which is intended to fetch the *full text*, the output I received was only the beginning of the book. This suggests the tool may have retrieved only a substantial initial portion, not the entire work (116,000 words). If you need the complete text, you will need to try accessing it through your licensed institutional ebook provider, as I cannot bypass DRM or access specific licensed databases directly.\n\nHere is the beginning of the text I retrieved:\n\n> **Day One.**\n>\n> I remember running. I remember the sound of my own breathing, ragged with panic, and the damp cold of the air against my face. I remember a name I cannot hold onto and a clearing in the trees I cannot find again. I remember screaming for someone called Anna, and Anna not coming. That is all I remember, and when I try to hold any of it for longer than a moment it slides out of reach, like a smooth stone sliding down a muddy bank.\n>\n> I find that I am standing at the edge of a wood. Mist clings to the lower branches; the trunks disappear upward into grey. Somewhere behind me a rook calls twice and stops. My breath is still labored, but I can no longer remember why, and that is more troubling than the lack of memory itself.\n>\n> There is a man beside me. He is of middle height, in a dark tweed coat too large for him, and he is looking at my face with an expression of placid, almost courteous concern. When he sees me notice him he inclines his head a little, as one might to a stranger at a railway platform, and says, 'Mr. Ashton, I think the best thing now is to walk back to the house.'\n>\n> Mr. Ashton. He means me. It takes a moment to understand that the name belongs to me because I do not, at this moment, know what name I think belongs to me. I try the word in my mouth silently and it sits there without meaning. Ashton. Edward Ashton? No, that isn't right either. I have the distinct sense that I am not Mr. Ashton at all, but that I am supposed to be, and that the man beside me believes without question that I am.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
